|~ `::span`, the kind of a stretch of source text, and what is declared
   under it: the words that read its ends.
   The decisions it exists for: [D249]. ~|

:span
  |~~ A span is a stretch of source text, the place where a reading
      stopped: six whole numbers under its tag, the offset from zero, the
      line and the column from one of its start, then of the point after
      its end, `::span[0 1 1 1 1 2]` for the first character of a text.
      `start` and `end` answer its ends as points.
      ::snippet~("~{" | parse !| get /location)
      ::link~(::point | doc) ::link~(::quote/ParseError | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | holds :PayloadNotSixNumbersError
        |~~ A span holds six whole numbers, the offset, the line and the
            column of its start and of its end.
        ~~|
        ~(allOf (count | eq 6) ~(every ~(is ::number)) ~(every ~(eq (floor))))
    | proves :PayloadNotSixNumbersError/refusesThreeNumbers
        |~~ Three numbers make a point, no span. ~~|
        ~(::span[0 1 1] !| type | eq ::span/PayloadNotSixNumbersError)

:span/start
  |~~ Answers the point the span starts at, the offset, the line and the
      column of its first character, `::span[0 1 1 4 1 5] | start`
      answering `::point[0 1 1]`.
      ::link~(::span/end | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The point the span starts at. ~~| ::point
    | throws :SubjectNotSpanError
        |~~ `start` reads the place a span starts at, as a parse error
            holds it, `"[1" | parse !| get /location | start`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersThePointItStartsAt
        |~~ The start of a span is the point of its first three numbers. ~~|
        ~(::span[0 1 1 4 1 5] | start | eq ::point[0 1 1])
    | impl ~(payload | take 3 | tag ::point)

:span/end
  |~~ Answers the point after the span's end, the offset, the line and
      the column past its last character, `::span[0 1 1 4 1 5] | end`
      answering `::point[4 1 5]`.
      ::link~(::span/start | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The point after the span's end. ~~| ::point
    | throws :SubjectNotSpanError
        |~~ `end` reads the place a span ends at, as a parse error holds
            it, `"[1" | parse !| get /location | end`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersThePointAfterIt
        |~~ The end of a span is the point of its last three numbers. ~~|
        ~(::span[0 1 1 4 1 5] | end | eq ::point[4 1 5])
    | impl ~(payload | drop 3 | tag ::point)

